// Dependency updates: one grouped pull request a week, merged by the bot when the gates are green.
//
// **A merge here is a production release.** A push to `main` migrates the production database and
// promotes the Vercel deployment (ADR-0019), so everything below is really about what is allowed
// to release unattended. Two things make that safe, and neither is automerge being careful:
//
//   - **The gates run before the release steps, in the same job.** A dependency that breaks the
//     build leaves `main` red and unreleased rather than shipping.
//   - **A release cannot carry an npm package published in the last three days.**
//     `security:minimumReleaseAgeNpm` below is the one that matters, and it is argued where it is
//     extended.
//
// The tuning is otherwise deliberately small. Everything not set is Renovate's own default, and
// the defaults were read from `lib/config/options/index.ts` on `renovatebot/renovate@main` rather
// than assumed. Settled by CAN-61 Keep the codebase and its dependencies from silting up.
{
  "$schema": "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",

  // Four presets, and the last three are the ticket's three requirements taken by name rather than
  // spelled out as cron and globs:
  //
  //   config:recommended   the dependency dashboard, and `group:monorepos` — which is what keeps
  //                        `react` and `react-dom` in one pull request rather than two that have
  //                        to land together
  //   schedule:weekly      -> schedule:earlyMondays -> ["* 0-3 * * 1"]
  //   group:allNonMajor    one pull request, "all non-major dependencies", matching minor + patch
  //   :automergeMinor      automerge patch and minor "if they pass tests"
  //
  // Those three expansions are quoted from the preset definitions themselves rather than from the
  // documentation site — `lib/config/presets/internal/{schedule,group,default}.preset.ts` — and each
  // was resolved against the installed package before being relied on here.
  //
  // Majors are deliberately in none of them: ungrouped, unautomerged, one pull request each for a
  // human. That is the point of grouping the rest — a major is the update worth reading.
  // A fifth joins them, and it is the one standing between automerge and an unattended production
  // deploy of a compromised package:
  //
  //   security:minimumReleaseAgeNpm    no npm release younger than three days is even raised
  //
  // **Automerging without a cooldown is a published attack path rather than a hypothetical one.**
  // In the `axios` 1.14.1 compromise the malicious package "was uploaded on March 31st at 00:20 am"
  // and "[o]nly 5 minutes later" a public repository's `package.json` had been modified; this "led
  // to the malicious package being pushed to production code in less than an hour, as showcased by
  // the jhipster/generator-jhipster repository", where "the upgrade was triggered at h+40mn and
  // merged at h+56mn. All this was allowed by a combination of Dependabot and an automerge workflow
  // in the CI/CD pipeline"
  // (https://blog.gitguardian.com/renovate-dependabot-the-new-malware-delivery-system/).
  //
  // **That was Dependabot rather than Renovate, and it does not matter**: what carried the malware
  // was the automerge workflow behind it, which is the shape this file configures. Three days is
  // that article's own floor for the fix — it prescribes `"minimumReleaseAge": "3 days"` for
  // Renovate by name — and the middle of the 3-to-7 the field settled on. It is also Renovate's own
  // choice, the preset existing to "allow for malware researchers and scanners to (possibly) detect
  // any malicious behaviour" (https://docs.renovatebot.com/presets-security/).
  //
  // **The preset rather than a bare `minimumReleaseAge: "3 days"`, and the difference is not
  // cosmetic.** Six update types cannot carry a release timestamp — `pin`, `lockFileMaintenance`,
  // `lockfileUpdate`, `rollback`, `bump` and `replacement` — and the default
  // `minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour` of `timestamp-required` treats a missing timestamp as an age not
  // yet reached, so a top-level setting holds all six *indefinitely*
  // (https://docs.renovatebot.com/key-concepts/minimum-release-age/). That is not academic here:
  // `config:recommended` pulls in `replacements:all`, so `replacement` updates do arise. The preset
  // opts each of the six out by name and attaches a note to the pull request saying so
  // (`lib/config/presets/internal/security.preset.ts`). A stall nobody is told about is the failure
  // this repository spends `scripts/check-docs.ts` avoiding; it should not be introduced here.
  //
  // **npm-only is the whole surface that automerges.** The other manager in this repository is
  // `github-actions`, and every pin there is either a major tag — `actions/checkout@v7` — or a
  // commit digest. From a major tag the only update Renovate can offer is `v7 -> v8`, a major; from
  // a digest it is a `digest` update. **Neither is a `minor` or a `patch`, and `:automergeMinor`
  // automerges nothing else**, so every Actions bump reaches a human regardless, which is what the
  // article's warning about workflow files asks for.
  //
  // The one digest is `millionco/react-doctor` in `.github/workflows/frontend.yml`, pinned by
  // CAN-60 Gate the front end on bytes, budgets and React lint. It is the first third-party Action
  // here — everything else is `actions/*` or `pnpm/*` — and a mutable major tag on a third party is
  // a supply-chain decision rather than a default. That workflow argues it beside the pin.
  //
  // **It costs nothing on a security fix.** `vulnerabilityAlerts` defaults to `minimumReleaseAge:
  // null` and `prCreation: "immediate"`, and "security updates bypass any `minimumReleaseAge`
  // checks" (same page) — so the delay applies to routine updates and never to the one class that
  // should not wait. `internalChecksFilter` is not set beside it either: the preset already sets
  // `strict` per rule, and that is the global default anyway.
  "extends": [
    "config:recommended",
    "schedule:weekly",
    "group:allNonMajor",
    ":automergeMinor",
    "security:minimumReleaseAgeNpm"
  ],

  // The cron in `schedule:weekly` is evaluated in this zone rather than UTC, so "before 4 AM" means
  // before 4 AM here. It matters twice a year and never otherwise.
  "timezone": "Europe/London",

  // `automergeSchedule` is deliberately absent, which leaves its default of "at any time": the
  // grouped pull request merges as soon as the two required contexts are green, whatever hour that
  // lands on. **This is a decision against the more common advice**, which is to hold the merge for
  // working hours so a broken release meets someone awake. It is taken knowingly: with the cooldown
  // above, the failure that window would catch is a legitimate update breaking the build — and that
  // one the gates already stop before the release steps run, leaving a red `main` rather than a bad
  // deploy. Waiting would buy a faster human response to a failure that does not reach production.
  //
  // Note that only automerge is unscheduled. `schedule:weekly` still confines branch and pull
  // request *creation* to Monday: out of schedule Renovate logs "Branch + PR exists but is not
  // scheduled -- will update if necessary" and carries on to the automerge check, which gates on
  // `automergeSchedule` alone (`lib/workers/repository/update/branch/index.ts` and
  // `.../update/pr/automerge.ts`). So green arriving after 4 AM does not stall the merge a week.

  // **This repository's commit subjects are prose, not Conventional Commits**
  // (docs/agents/workflow.md -> The loop), and squash-merge takes the pull request title as the
  // subject, so a bot writing `chore(deps): …` would put the one shape this repo does not use onto
  // `main`. The default is `auto`, which reads recent history, would find no semantic commits and
  // switch itself off — the right answer reached by a heuristic. Disabling it outright makes the
  // repository's own rule binding rather than incidental.
  "semanticCommits": "disabled",

  // **Renovate merges, not GitHub.** `platformAutomerge` defaults to `true`, and this repository
  // has GitHub's auto-merge feature switched off (`allow_auto_merge: false`), which Renovate
  // handles by logging `GitHub-native automerge: not enabled in repo settings` at debug and
  // carrying on with its own merge (`lib/modules/platform/github/index.ts`, `tryPrAutomerge`).
  // That is the right outcome reached silently, in a log nobody reads. Saying it here makes the
  // config describe what actually happens.
  //
  // Nothing is given up by it. `main`'s ruleset requires two named contexts and has no bypass
  // actor, and Renovate "will not automerge until it sees passing status checks / check runs for
  // the branch" (https://docs.renovatebot.com/key-concepts/automerge/); a merge GitHub refuses for
  // a missing check is logged and retried on the next run rather than forced.
  "platformAutomerge": false,

  // Squash is the only method this repository offers, and `main` requires linear history. The
  // default `auto` would infer it, and Renovate's own fallback tries squash first anyway, so this
  // changes no behaviour — it removes a guess from the path that lands commits on `main`.
  "automergeStrategy": "squash"
}
